{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 18, "model_name": "meta/llama-4-maverick-17b-128e-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a nobleman and leader of the County of Tripoli from 1152 to 1187. He was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. *## Early Life and Regency* Raymond III was born around 1140 and became Count of Tripoli at the age of 12 after his father's assassination in 1152. As he was a minor his mother Hodierna acted as regent until he came of age. \n\nAfter Hodierna's regency ended Raymond III took part in the failed siege of Ascalon in 1153 alongside King Baldwin III of Jerusalem. In 1161 Raymond III was taken captive by Nur ad-Din Zangi while raiding the Beqa'a Valley and was held prisoner for nearly a decade. He was released in 1174 after a ransom was paid. \n\nUpon his release Raymond III became regent for King Baldwin IV of Jerusalem who was suffering from leprosy. *### Regency for Baldwin IV* As regent Raymond III played a crucial role in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem and worked to secure a truce with Sultan Saladin of Egypt. He was a member of the council that advised Baldwin IV and helped to secure the succession of Baldwin V. \n\nIn 1180 Raymond III's regency ended as Baldwin IV came of age and he was replaced by Guy of Lusignan. Raymond III then allied himself with Bohemond III of Antioch and Prince Rupen III of Armenia. *#### The Battle of Hattin* In 1187 Raymond III led the forces of Tripoli at the Battle of Hattin against Saladin. The battle was a decisive defeat for the Crusaders and Raymond III was one of the few leaders who managed to escape. \n\nAfter the Battle of Hattin Raymond III retreated to Tripoli and died there in 1187. He was succeeded by his godson Raymond IV who was the son of Bohemond III of Antioch. Raymond III was known for his military prowess and his leadership skills and is considered one of the most important figures in the history of the County of